Collins was approached to write the title song to the American [[neo-noir]] film  ''[[Against All Odds (1984 film)|Against All Odds]]'' (1984) while it was still in its preliminary "[[rough cut]] form".<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Morris|first=Chris|title=Early Music-Film Ties Best|magazine=Billboard|date=6 December 1986|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=NUUEAAAAMBAJ&pg=RA1-PA77|access-date=15 March 2016|archive-date=15 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230115151848/https://books.google.com/books?id=NUUEAAAAMBAJ&pg=RA1-PA77|url-status=live}}</ref> Director [[Taylor Hackford]], who previously used [[Up Where We Belong|a song]] for ''[[An Officer and a Gentleman]]'' (1982), planned to do the same for ''Against All Odds'',{{sfn|Denisoff|Romanowski|1991|p=406}} which is a remake of ''[[Out of the Past]]'' (1947). When he signed with [[Atlantic Records]], he was provided with a roster of artists, among whom Collins was chosen to render the film's theme song based on the quality of his voice.{{sfn|Denisoff|Romanowski|1991|p=406}} 

At the time the soundtrack was being completed, Collins was on tour with [[Genesis (band)|Genesis]]. Hackford flew to [[Chicago]] to see one of the band's concerts. Collins watched the film on a [[videocassette recorder]] in his hotel room and agreed to appear on the soundtrack.<ref name="HoganAllInfo">{{cite web|last=Hogan|first=Ed|title=Against All Odds (Take a Look at Me Now)|url=http://www.allmusic.com/song/against-all-odds-take-a-look-at-me-now-t2760660|work=Allmusic|publisher=Rovi Corporation|access-date=3 September 2011|archive-date=25 May 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110525011108/http://www.allmusic.com/song/against-all-odds-take-a-look-at-me-now-t2760660|url-status=live}}</ref> Hackford said that it was a "textbook case of designing a song to reflect what the film is".{{sfn|Denisoff|Romanowski|1991|p=406}} The song appears in the film as background music during the closing credits.{{sfn|Denisoff|Romanowski|1991|p=408}}

Originally titled "How Can You Just Sit There?", the song's music was written by Collins during sessions for his debut solo album ''[[Face Value (album)|Face Value]]'' (1981), but it became discarded as it was his least favourite of the several ballads he wrote at the time.<ref name=facevalue>Tobler, John. [http://www.genesis-path.net/art87-03.html "The Progressive Reign of Genesis"]  {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20050825213838/http://www.genesis-path.net/art87-03.html |date=25 August 2005 }}. ''Billboard Magazine''. 7 March 1987.</ref><ref name=MusicianJun85>{{cite magazine|title=The Second Coming of Phil Collins|first=JD|last=Considine|author-link=JD Considine|date=June 1985|magazine=Musician}}</ref> Nor was it included on ''[[Hello, I Must Be Going! (album)|Hello, I Must Be Going!]]'' (1982), as many newer songs were written for it. Collins eventually presented the demo to Hackford when approached to write a song for ''Against All Odds'', which he loved. Collins then penned the lyrics, which were for the film.<ref name=MusicianJun85 /> Due to lack of time available (as he was on tour with Genesis), he had [[arrangement|arranger]] [[Arif Mardin]] produce it, and they worked on it over two days.<ref name=MusicianJun85 /> The piano performance is by New York musician [[Rob Mounsey]]. Piano, keyboard bass and a string section arranged and conducted by Mardin were recorded at RCA Studios, New York, while Collins recorded vocals and drums in Los Angeles.{{sfn|Denisoff|Romanowski|1991|p=406}}

On episode 339 of ''This American Life'', "Break Up", Collins relays that the song was inspired and written shortly after the breakup between him and his first wife. In the interview he says that the divorce transformed him from being a musician into also being a lyricist.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thisamericanlife.org/339/break-up|title=Break-Up|date=24 August 2007|website=This American Life|access-date=14 August 2026}}</ref>

The song was first included on a Collins album on the 1998 compilation ''[[Hits (Phil Collins album)|Hits]]'', and it also appeared on his compilation ''[[Love Songs: A Compilation... Old and New]]'' (2004). A live performance of the song also appears on the ''[[Serious Hits... Live!]]'' album. In 2015, Collins released the original demo recording from the ''Face Value'' sessions as part of his ''Take A Look At Me Now'' project.

